<article class="env-proposition" id="3DFE8F">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/3DFE8F">Proposition <span data-tag="3DFE8F">2.4.2.8</span>.</a> Let $p: X \rightarrow S$ be a locally Cartesian fibration. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The map $p$ is a Cartesian fibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
Given a $2$-simplex 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { x \ar [rr]^{f} \ar [dr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  x' \ar [dl]^{g} \\ &amp;  z, &amp;  } \]
</div>
<p>
 if $f$ and $g$ are locally $p$-Cartesian, then $h$ is locally $p$-Cartesian. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
Every locally $p$-Cartesian edge of $X$ is $p$-Cartesian. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The equivalence $(2) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/323464" data-tag="323464">2.4.2.7</a>, and the implication $(3) \Rightarrow (1)$ is obvious. To prove that $(1) \Rightarrow (3)$, let us suppose that $e: x \rightarrow y$ is a locally $p$-Cartesian edge of $X$. Choose a $p$-Cartesian edge $e': x' \rightarrow y$ lifting $p(e)$. The edges $e$ and $e'$ are both $p'$-Cartesian in $X' = X \times _{S} \Delta ^1$, where $p': X' \rightarrow \Delta ^1$ denotes the projection. It follows that $e$ and $e'$ are equivalent in $X'$, and therefore also equivalent in $X$. Since $e'$ is $p$-Cartesian, we deduce that $e$ is $p$-Cartesian as well. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>